/**
 * UpdatePurchaseOrderLineRequest
 *
 * Partial update of ONE line of a purchase order that still accepts changes. Every field is optional and an empty body is valid: it changes nothing and is not an error. Of a purchase line you edit the QUANTITY, and only the quantity: `quantity` is the new ordered quantity in PURCHASE UNIT — the one published by the offer the line was copied from — greater than zero, with at most four decimals and up to 9999999999.9999; it does not accept `null`, because a line with no quantity does not exist, and to leave it as it is you simply omit it. Changing the product, the variant or the offer is NOT editing a line: it is removing it and adding another. The line keeps the agreed terms — supplier reference, purchase unit, conversion factor, cost, minimum quantity and lead time — and they are copied ALL AT ONCE from a single offer only when the line is added; accepting a new product here would leave a line with the unit of one offer and the cost of another. That is why `product_id`, `product_variant_id` and `offer_id` are rejected naming the field, with the alternative in the message, instead of being ignored. With the same criterion this body rejects the unit cost (`unit_cost` and `unit_price`) and the three percentages — the ones of the copied offer —, the `description` — composed from the product —, the `warehouse_id` — fixed when the line is added —, the line `id` — which is the one in the URL, not a field of the body — and the `received_quantity`, which is never writable on any route. The line amount and the taxes derived from it have their own limit, checked against the cost the line already holds before the change is applied. Lowering the quantity below what has already been received, or editing a line of an order whose status no longer allows changes, is rejected as a business-rule violation and not as a validation error.
 */
export type UpdatePurchaseOrderLineRequest = {
    /**
     * Not accepted. The line is identified by the URL and its identity is assigned by the order.
     */
    id?: string;
    /**
     * Not accepted: the product is the IDENTITY of the line. Remove the line and add another, so the purchase unit and the cost are copied together from its offer.
     */
    product_id?: string;
    /**
     * Not accepted: like the product, the variant is identity of the line.
     */
    product_variant_id?: string;
    /**
     * Not accepted: changing the offer would leave the unit of one offer with the cost of another.
     */
    offer_id?: string;
    /**
     * Not accepted: the description is composed from the product being ordered.
     */
    description?: string;
    /**
     * New ordered quantity in PURCHASE UNIT — the one published by the offer the line was copied from. Greater than zero, at most four decimals, up to 9999999999.9999. It does not accept `null`: a line with no quantity does not exist; omit the key to leave it as it is. Lowering it below what has already been received is rejected as a business-rule violation.
     */
    quantity?: number;
    /**
     * Not accepted: the warehouse of the line is fixed when the line is added.
     */
    warehouse_id?: string;
    /**
     * Not accepted: the unit cost is copied from the supplier offer. Pick another offer, or send the lines with their cost when you create the order.
     */
    unit_cost?: string;
    /**
     * Not accepted: the unit cost is copied from the supplier offer. Pick another offer, or send the lines with their cost when you create the order.
     */
    unit_price?: string;
    /**
     * Not accepted: the tax, withholding and surcharge percentages of the line are taken from the product and the supplier offer.
     */
    tax_rate?: string;
    /**
     * Not accepted: the tax, withholding and surcharge percentages of the line are taken from the product and the supplier offer.
     */
    retention_rate?: string;
    /**
     * Not accepted: the tax, withholding and surcharge percentages of the line are taken from the product and the supplier offer.
     */
    surcharge_rate?: string;
    /**
     * Not accepted on any route: the received quantity is DERIVED from posted goods receipts and moves on its own when one is posted or cancelled.
     */
    received_quantity?: string;
};

/**
 * UpdatePurchaseOrderRequest
 *
 * Partial update of the HEADER of a purchase order. Every field is optional and an empty body is valid: it changes nothing and is not an error. What you do not send keeps its current value and what you send as `null` is CLEARED, which is how you remove the expected date or the destination warehouse without touching anything else. Writable: `supplier_order_reference`, `warehouse_id`, `expected_date` and `notes`. What is not writable here: the SUPPLIER, which is the identity of the order — buying from somebody else is another order, not an edit of this one —; the LINES, which have their own sub-resource, so adding, changing or removing one does not mean resending the rest; the NUMBER, stamped at creation; and the STATUS, which the transition operations move. Reusing a `supplier_order_reference` another order of your company already holds is rejected with a conflict, exactly like at creation. An order whose status no longer admits editing rejects this operation as a business-rule violation, with 422 and never 403.
 */
export type UpdatePurchaseOrderRequest = {
    /**
     * Reference the supplier uses for this order in its own system, up to 120 characters. `null` clears it, and reusing one that another order of your company already holds is rejected with a conflict.
     */
    supplier_order_reference?: string | null;
    /**
     * Public ID of an ACTIVE warehouse of your company the goods are expected at. `null` removes it.
     */
    warehouse_id?: string | null;
    /**
     * Date the goods are expected (`YYYY-MM-DD`). `null` clears it.
     */
    expected_date?: string | null;
    /**
     * Free text attached to the order, up to 1000 characters. `null` clears it.
     */
    notes?: string | null;
};

